latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the input grid:\n   a. Get the number of rows and columns of the input grid.\n   b. Determine the larger dimension (max of rows and columns).\n\n2. Calculate the expansion factor:\n   a. The expansion factor is equal to the larger dimension.\n   b. This ensures the output will be square and each dimension will be a multiple of the input dimensions.\n\n3. Create the expanded grid:\n   a. Initialize a new empty grid with dimensions: (rows * expansion_factor) x (columns * expansion_factor).\n\n4. Fill the expanded grid:\n   a. Iterate through each cell of the new grid.\n   b. For each cell (i, j) in the new grid:\n      - Calculate the corresponding cell in the original grid:\n        original_row = i % original_rows\n        original_col = j % original_columns\n      - Copy the color value from the original grid to the new grid.\n\n5. Return the new expanded grid.\n\nHere's how we would implement this in Python:\n\n```python\nfrom rob_agi.colored_grid import ColoredGrid\n\ndef solve_ccd554ac(input_grid: ColoredGrid) -> ColoredGrid:\n    # Step 1: Analyze the input grid\n    original_rows, original_cols = input_grid.get_dimensions()\n    \n    # Step 2: Calculate the expansion factor\n    expansion_factor = max(original_rows, original_cols)\n    \n    # Step 3: Create the expanded grid\n    new_rows = original_rows * expansion_factor\n    new_cols = original_cols * expansion_factor\n    new_grid = [[0 for _ in range(new_cols)] for _ in range(new_rows)]\n    \n    # Step 4: Fill the expanded grid\n    for i in range(new_rows):\n        for j in range(new_cols):\n            original_row = i % original_rows\n            original_col = j % original_cols\n            new_grid[i][j] = input_grid.values[original_row][original_col]\n    \n    # Step 5: Return the new expanded grid\n    return ColoredGrid(values=new_grid)\n```\n\nThis implementation should work for all the given examples and test cases. It takes the input grid, determines how many times it needs to be repeated based on its largest dimension, and then creates a new grid by tiling the original pattern.\n\nThe key insight is using the modulo operation to map each cell in the new grid back to its corresponding cell in the original grid. This allows us to repeat the pattern seamlessly in both directions.\n\nThis solution is flexible enough to handle input grids of various sizes and aspect ratios, always producing a square output grid that's a multiple of the input dimensions."
